\tag{fdip}
\title{Gluing and spin networks}
\date{2026-11-03}

\p{Can the physics on a closed surface be built by joining the
physics of its pieces?}

\p{Attaching a handle composes boundary counts by convolution of
class functions. The closed count is the value of that convolution
at the identity. The normalized flat partition function of a
cellulation of genus #{g} is that value divided by #{|G|^{2g-1}}.
On a finite graph, invariant tensors at the vertices span the
gauge-invariant functions, and the dimension of those functions is
the number of gauge orbits.}

\p{The papers that present finite-group gauge theory as the
two-dimensional theory of the center of the group algebra have not
been read. This chapter does not identify the handle convolution
with that theory or with the spatial transfer operator. No
continuous spin-network basis is claimed.}

\transclude{fdip-00A8}
